					<span class="printuser deleted" data-id="1361261"><img class="small" src="http://www.wikidot.com/common--images/avatars/default/a16.png" alt=""/>(account deleted)</span> <span class="odate time_1421971551 format_%25e%20%25b%20%25Y%2C%20%25H%3A%25M%7Cagohover">23 Jan 2015 00:05</span>
														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-2214254">
									

<p>Not bad. Enough detail to sell the phenomenon as a genuinely documented scientific phenomenon, without going overboard and boring the reader with extraneous material. It hits a sweet spot in terms of tone, dry enough to help sell the more unsettling bits of the log without treading into &quot;oooOOooo boogedy boooo&quot; territory. Good work.</p>
<p>Two issues though, which while not affecting my overall vote I would really like to see addressed:</p>
<ul>
<li>How can they extract DNA from the molars of the skeletons if they're all headless?</li>
<li>There is no reason whatsoever for that Daevite reference to be in there. I understand that crosslinking is being pushed now in some quarters, and that's fine and all, but I would prefer that stuff like that serve some sort of purpose. This article is strong enough to stand on its own; I see no reason to gratuitously throw cross-references in there.</li>
</ul>

							</div>

								
					
